KEY POINTS
- In an if-else condition, if the given input is non-zero the condition is TRUE(1), else the condition is FALSE(0).
- We can overwrite the value in if condition even if the number is already defined before.
eg: int n = 10;
    if(n = 2)
output: n =2
- without "{}" if conditions will be printed only if there is a single statement.
- if we are giving group of statements in the if or else conditions then "{}" are MANDATORY.
;- should not print after the if/if-else/else-if/nested if Conditions.
- even : n%2== 0
-odd: n%2 !=0/n%2 == 1
Nested-if Condition syntax:
if(condition 1)
{
  if (condition 2)
{
//statement
}
}
-Executes only if the both conditions are TRUE.

NESTED IF-ELSE CONDITION:
if(c1)
{
if(c2)
{
//statement 1
}
else
{
//statement 2
}
}
else
{
if(c3)
{
//statement 3
}
else
{
//statement 4
}
}

SWITCH: syntax
switch(expression)
{
case label 1:
//statement 1
break;
case label 2:
//statement 2
break;
case label 3:
//statement 3
break;
default:
//statement 4
break;
}
- n no. of case labels can be given.
-break = used to come out of the program
-fall through condition: when the break is not mentioned, it goes to the next case labels until break statement/end of the program.
-syntax order is not important.
-duplicate values are not allowed.
-switch case is only applicable to int, char.
-for float, double switch case cannot be used.
